WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.153
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.453 --> 00:00:09.141
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.441 --> 00:00:16.900
Unit sixteen: Structuralism. Today you will describe hidden structures with inverted 'beneath / underlying' sentences.

4
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.887
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:24.368
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.217 --> 00:00:26.241
Structure.

7
00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:30.109
Structure means the way parts are arranged to form a whole.

8
00:00:30.717 --> 00:00:33.436
The structure of the myth repeats across cultures.

9
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.668
Your turn. Say: Structure.

10
00:00:39.533 --> 00:00:40.511
Structure.

11
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:42.739
Binary opposition.

12
00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.805
Binary opposition means a pair of opposite concepts such as light/dark.

13
00:00:48.417 --> 00:00:51.391
Good versus evil is a basic binary opposition.

14
00:00:51.983 --> 00:00:55.448
Your turn. Say: Binary opposition.

15
00:00:58.300 --> 00:00:59.765
Binary opposition.

16
00:01:00.517 --> 00:01:01.483
System.

17
00:01:02.483 --> 00:01:05.586
System means a set of connected elements working together.

18
00:01:06.183 --> 00:01:08.264
Language is a system of differences.

19
00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.421
Your turn. Say: System.

20
00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:14.931
System.

21
00:01:15.683 --> 00:01:17.126
Underlying pattern.

22
00:01:18.117 --> 00:01:22.160
Underlying pattern means a hidden regularity beneath surface variety.

23
00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:25.260
An underlying pattern links the folk tales.

24
00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.491
Your turn. Say: Underlying pattern.

25
00:01:31.333 --> 00:01:32.927
Underlying pattern.

26
00:01:33.683 --> 00:01:34.371
Langue.

27
00:01:35.383 --> 00:01:38.335
Langue means language as an abstract shared system.

28
00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:41.669
Langue includes the grammar every speaker knows.

29
00:01:42.283 --> 00:01:45.261
Your turn. Say: Langue.

30
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:49.008
Langue.

31
00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:50.530
Parole.

32
00:01:51.517 --> 00:01:54.399
Parole means individual acts of speech or writing.

33
00:01:54.983 --> 00:01:57.633
Each conversation is an example of parole.

34
00:01:58.217 --> 00:02:00.289
Your turn. Say: Parole.

35
00:02:03.133 --> 00:02:04.088
Parole.

36
00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:05.932
Universal.

37
00:02:06.917 --> 00:02:10.042
Universal means applying to all cases or cultures.

38
00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:13.335
Structuralists searched for universal patterns.

39
00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.602
Your turn. Say: Universal.

40
00:02:19.467 --> 00:02:20.514
Universal.

41
00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:22.158
Relation.

42
00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:26.102
Relation means the way two or more things are connected.

43
00:02:26.717 --> 00:02:29.541
Meaning arises from relations between signs.

44
00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.547
Your turn. Say: Relation.

45
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:36.529
Relation.

46
00:02:37.333 --> 00:02:41.135
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:41.983 --> 00:02:47.912
Visal, you analysed three Cambodian folk tales. They seem completely different. What did you find?

48
00:02:49.017 --> 00:02:58.702
Beneath the different plots lies a single structural pattern: a weak hero, a test, a helper, and a reversal of fortune.

49
00:02:59.817 --> 00:03:01.677
And the binary oppositions?

50
00:03:02.783 --> 00:03:12.550
Underlying every tale is the opposition of village and forest: order and safety on one side, danger and transformation on the other.

51
00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.149
Isn't that reducing rich stories to a formula?

52
00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:25.823
That's the main criticism. Structuralism reveals the system, but it can ignore the individual voice of each storyteller.

53
00:03:26.967 --> 00:03:29.047
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:29.883 --> 00:03:33.764
Grammar focus: Beneath and underlying descriptive language.

55
00:03:34.064 --> 00:03:35.285
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:35.983 --> 00:03:38.610
Beneath the plot lies a deeper structural pattern.

57
00:03:39.517 --> 00:03:43.397
Underlying every tale is the opposition of village and forest.

58
00:03:44.283 --> 00:03:47.572
Behind the variety of stories stands a single system.

59
00:03:48.533 --> 00:03:58.674
Fronting a place phrase and inverting the verb ('Beneath the plot lies a pattern') delays the key idea for emphasis. The verb agrees with the subject that follows it: lies a pattern / lie patterns.

60
00:03:59.417 --> 00:04:03.358
Be careful! Don't say: Beneath the plot lie a deeper pattern.

61
00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:08.159
Say: Beneath the plot lies a deeper pattern.

62
00:04:08.459 --> 00:04:10.934
After inversion, the verb agrees with 'pattern'.

63
00:04:11.733 --> 00:04:14.362
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:15.167 --> 00:04:16.528
The Grammar of Stories.

65
00:04:16.828 --> 00:04:23.123
Structuralism proposes that meaning arises not from individual elements but from their relations within a system.

66
00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:29.558
Saussure distinguished langue, the shared system of a language, from parole, its individual use.

67
00:04:30.567 --> 00:04:37.059
Applying this idea to myths, Lévi-Strauss argued that beneath their surface variety lie recurring binary oppositions.

68
00:04:38.067 --> 00:04:44.931
Underlying a vast range of folk tales, Propp identified a limited set of functions, such as the test and the reward.

69
00:04:45.933 --> 00:04:52.287
Critics later objected that this search for universal structures overlooked history, context and individual creativity.

70
00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:57.111
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:01.041
Beneath the surface blank several hidden patterns.

72
00:05:01.341 --> 00:05:04.669
A, lies. B, lie. C, lying.

73
00:05:08.233 --> 00:05:10.256
The answer is B: lie.

74
00:05:11.417 --> 00:05:14.008
Light/dark and good/evil are examples of blank.

75
00:05:14.308 --> 00:05:18.960
A, binary oppositions. B, paroles. C, universals.

76
00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:25.405
The answer is A: binary oppositions.

77
00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:28.747
An individual act of speech is blank.

78
00:05:29.047 --> 00:05:32.712
A, langue. B, system. C, parole.

79
00:05:36.267 --> 00:05:38.406
The answer is C: parole.

80
00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:41.770
Choose the correct inverted sentence.

81
00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:49.648
A, Underlying the tale is a conflict.. B, Underlying the tale a conflict is.. C, Underlying is the tale a conflict..

82
00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.558
The answer is A: Underlying the tale is a conflict..

83
00:05:57.717 --> 00:06:01.655
According to the reading, what did critics say structuralism overlooked?

84
00:06:01.955 --> 00:06:08.093
A, binary oppositions. B, history, context and creativity. C, the shared system.

85
00:06:11.633 --> 00:06:15.014
The answer is B: history, context and creativity.

86
00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:19.305
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:23.520
What binary opposition can you find in a story you know well?

88
00:06:40.317 --> 00:06:41.887
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:42.187 --> 00:07:19.027
In the story of Tum Teav, which every Cambodian student knows, the central binary opposition is love versus social duty. Underlying the plot is the conflict between personal desire and the power of family and status. Tum and Teav choose love, while Teav's mother and the governor represent rigid social order. Some readers prefer to see the tale simply as a tragic romance. However, recognising the opposition helps me understand why the story still feels relevant today.

90
00:07:19.327 --> 00:07:20.978
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:21.883 --> 00:07:26.951
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and beneath and underlying descriptive language.

92
00:07:27.251 --> 00:07:31.749
Today's value is analytical thinking. Look for the pattern underneath the surface.

93
00:07:32.049 --> 00:07:34.748
See you in Unit seventeen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:35.048 --> 00:07:37.700
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

